 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Computational Cost:** Significantly reduced compared to full-resolution rendering. Rendering a full image can take tens of seconds per iteration; strided sampling with $s=2$ reduces this cost to a fraction, enabling efficient consistency regularization during training [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Accuracy:** The method relies on the accuracy of the rendered depth. Low-resolution depth may lack fine details, potentially causing warping artifacts in high-frequency regions. Bilinear upsampling helps mitigate this but does not recover true high-frequency geometry [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Accuracy:** The method relies on the accuracy of the rendered depth. Low-resolution depth may lack fine details, potentially causing warping artifacts in high-frequency regions [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Use Case:** Best suited for few-shot NeRF training where geometric consistency is critical and full-resolution rendering is too slow for frequent consistency checks [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Rendered Depth-Guided Warping was introduced in the context of GeCoNeRF (Geometric Consistency NeRF) for few-shot neural radiance fields [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the computational bottleneck of rendering full images for consistency regularization by leveraging low-resolution depth maps and inverse warping [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the alpha compositing principles of [NeRF Rendering (Alpha Compositing)](5124-nerf-rendering-alpha-compositing.md) to generate the depth priors necessary for the warping process [S1, unofficial].
